.\" Manpage for cloneup.sh.
.TH CLONEUP.SH 1 "30 September 2024" "1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cloneup.sh \- clone a drive using \fBrsync\fR(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cloneup.sh \fR[\fIOPTION\fR]... 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Source and destination drives need not be the same size as long as all source data fits on destination. If the source drive is bootable the destination will become bootable provided certain conditions are met (see \fBLIMITS\fR).

Multiple sessions can run in parallel, e.g., in different terminals, provided the destination drives are (obviously) different. For example, if three drives are connected to the PC one drive can be cloned to the other two simultaneously.

During execution you can cancel anytime with Ctrl-C.

If run with no command line arguments, a list of files/dirs that are excluded from cloning is displayed, e.g., system files under \fI/dev/*\fR, \fI/proc/*\fR, \fI/sys/*\fR, etc. These files/dirs are read from the configuration file \fIexclude.conf\fR (see \fBFILES\fR). Also, a list of drives connected to the PC is displayed and user input is required to select source and destination drives. Alternatively, the user can supply command line options (see \fBOPTIONS\fR), e.g., source and destination drives. However, this is unintuitive but useful to run in a script.

During cloning feedback is provided via standard output. For example, \fBrsync\fR(1) provides a progress indicator which includes total bytes and percentage cloned. Log files are created under \fI/var/log/cloneup.sh.d/\fR (see \fBFILES\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
If run with no command line arguments, a user-friendly menu is displayed with the connected drives prefixed by sequence number. Then, just input source and destination drive numbers separated by a space, e.g.,: 1 2.
.SH FILES
The default configuration files are under \fI/etc/cloneup.sh.d/\fR:
    \fIexclude.conf\fR (files/dirs to exclude from cloning, see \fBcloneup.sh-exclude.conf\fR(5))
    \fIfstypes.conf\fR (contains commands to format a partition for various filesystems)

The user can create new configuration files under \fI/etc/cloneup.sh.d/conf.d/\fR and/or \fI~/.config/cloneup.sh.d/\fR but the default filenames (see above) have to be used. Files under \fI/etc/cloneup.sh.d/conf.d/\fR modify (override) the default ones and files under \fI~/.config/cloneup.sh.d/\fR replace the default ones. It is recommended to not modify the default \fIfstypes.conf\fR file or create your own unless you really know what you are doing.

If not run under its directory (\fI/usr/sbin/\fR) the configuration files under \fI~/.config/cloneup.sh.d/\fR are used (if they exist). In any other case the configuration files under \fI/etc/cloneup.sh.d/\fR and \fI/etc/cloneup.sh.d/conf.d/\fR are used.

Configuration files can also be provided when supplying command line options (see \fBOPTIONS\fR). They take priority over any other files and any valid pathnames can be used.

Unless the dry run command line option is used (see \fBOPTIONS\fR), every time you run a cloning session the log directory \fI/var/log/cloneup.sh.d/<x>_<y>/\fR is deleted and then created. \fI<x>\fR and \fI<y>\fR are the numbers of the source and destination drives respectively (user input). The following files are created under this directory:
    \fIlog\fR      (contains files/dirs cloned)
    \fIerrors\fR   (contains any runtime errors)
    \fIcommands\fR (contains most commands used)

If the dry run command line option is used (see \fBOPTIONS\fR), the log directory (see above) is not deleted and all files under it are appended to.

Sometimes it is useful to \fBtail\fR(1) the \fIlog\fR file to see which files/dirs are being transferred by \fBrsync\fR(1) and to ensure that \fBrsync\fR(1) is not stuck.

If multiple clone sessions are executed in parallel a log directory per session is created.

Before cloning begins the lock file \fI/var/lock/cloneup.sh.d/pids\fR is created to ensure that no other cloning session will use the same destination. The lock file is deleted after all cloning sessions have completed.
.SH OPTIONS
\fB-d\fR, \fB--dst\fR \fIdestination_drive\fR
    destination drive in the form /dev/<drv>, e.g. /dev/sdb (must be combined with '\fB-s\fR|\fB--src\fR' option)

\fB-e\fR, \fB--exclude\fR \fIexclude_file\fR
    takes priority over other exclude files (see \fBFILES\fR) and must be a valid pathname

\fB-f\fR, \fB--fstypes\fR \fIfstypes_file\fR
    takes priority over other filesystem type files (see \fBFILES\fR) and must be a valid pathname

\fB-h\fR, \fB--help\fR
    prints a short help message

\fB-r\fR, \fB--dry-run\fR
    prints commands to be used during cloning without executing them (this will append to the log files, see \fBFILES\fR, from the previous run if source and destination are the same)

\fB-s\fR, \fB--src\fR \fIsource_drive\fR
    source drive in the form /dev/<drv>, e.g. /dev/sda (must be combined with '\fB-d\fR|\fB--dst\fR' option)
.SH LIMITS
Must run as root.

If init is not \fBsystemd\fR(1), system sleep (suspend/hibernate) won't be inhibited. Thus, if system sleep is enabled and activated during cloning, cloning will be interrupted.

The source drive must not use Logical Volume Management (\fBlvm\fR(8)).

The source drive must have partitions with UUIDs.

If the source drive has partitions with the btrfs filesystem any subvolumes on them will not be created on the destination. To see which filesystems are fully supported check the \fIfstypes.conf\fR file (see \fBFILES\fR).

If the source drive is bootable the destination will become bootable only if the source drive is using the GRUB 2 bootloader. Regardless, the destination will contain all source data provided cloning was successful.

.SH EXIT_STATUS
Zero on success else > 1. In case of error an appropriate error message is displayed and further info might be available in the error file (see \fBFILES\fR).
.SH NOTES
The \fBrsync\fR(1) \fI--inc-recursive\fR (\fI--i-r\fR) command line option is used to use less memory. However, this option interferes with the \fBrsync\fR(1) \fI--progress\fR command line option which is also used. Unfortunately, the percentage of files transferred that is displayed is incorrect but the data size is not.
.SH CAVEATS
If third programs are running during a cloning session then it's possible they will delete some of their working files, e.g., temp files. However, these files are part of \fBrsync\fR(1)'s list of files to transfer. Hence, when \fBrsync\fR(1) exits, its return code is 24 (partial transfer due to vanished source files) which will be ignored. It is recommended that prior to cloning, all programs on all user accounts are closed and all user accounts are exited except the one used for cloning.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBcloneup.sh-exclude.conf\fR(5), \fBrsync\fR(1), \fBsystemd\fR(1), \fBlvm\fR(8), \fBgrub-install\fR(8), \fBgrub-mkconfig\fR(8)
